In this error, we can see that there is an `InputMismatchException` being thrown when attempting to read integer input using the `Scanner` class in Java. This exception is thrown when the next token (in this case, an integer) cannot be converted into an int.

To fix this issue, you can add input validation to ensure that the user enters a valid integer before proceeding with further calculations. Here's an example implementation:

```java
import java.util.InputMismatchException;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        int num1 = 0;
        int num2 = 0;

        System.out.println("Enter first number: ");
        while (true) {
            try {
                num1 = scanner.nextInt();
                break;
            } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
                System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter an integer.");
                scanner.nextLine(); // consume invalid input
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Enter second number: ");
        while (true) {
            try {
                num2 = scanner.nextInt();
                break;
            } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
                System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter an integer.");
                scanner.nextLine(); // consume invalid input
            }
        }

        int sum = num1 + num2;
        System.out.println("Sum: " + sum);
    }
}
```

In this updated version of the code, I added two `while` loops to repeatedly prompt the user for input until a valid integer is entered. Inside each loop, I use a `try-catch` block to handle `InputMismatchException` and consume any remaining input using `scanner.nextLine()`. By doing so, we avoid infinite loops and ensure that only valid input is used in our calculation.

