Auth0's Single Sign-On (SSO) feature allows you to easily implement centralized authentication and authorization across multiple React applications. With Auth0 SSO, users can seamlessly switch between your different web and mobile apps without the need to re-enter their credentials each time. This streamlined user experience improves security and convenience while reducing development costs associated with managing multiple authentication systems.

Here's how you can set up Auth0 SSO for multiple React apps:

1. Create an Auth0 account or log in if you already have one.
2. Create a new application by navigating to the Applications dashboard and clicking on "Create Application". Choose the "Single Page App" option and provide your app's name and callback URL.
3. Generate a Client Secret key and save it securely as you won't be able to view it again later.
4. Configure the SSO settings by going to the Application Settings tab and selecting "Enable SSO" under the Advanced tab. Follow the prompts to configure your SSO domain and metadata.
5. Repeat steps 1-4 for all of your React apps that will participate in the SSO configuration.
6. In each of your React apps, install the `@auth0/auth0-spa-js` library and create a new `auth0-variables.js` file to store your Auth0 client ID and domain.
7. Initialize Auth0 in your main entry point (usually `index.js`) by importing `auth0-variables.js` and creating a new `auth0Client` instance.
8. Protect your routes using Auth0's `useAuth0()` hook, which automatically handles redirecting users to the appropriate login page based on the configured SSO settings.

Here's an example of how to protect a route using `useAuth0()`:

```jsx
import { useAuth0 } from '@auth0/auth0-spa-js';

const MyProtectedComponent = () => {
  const { user, isAuthenticated } = useAuth0();

  return (
    <div>
      {isAuthenticated && <h1>{user.email}</h1>}
      {!isAuthenticated && <Redirect to="/login"/>}
    </div>
  );
};

Here's the C code snippet to convert `argv` (command-line arguments) to a null-terminated string (C string):

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

char *join_args(int argc, char **argv) {
    size_t len = 0;
    for (int I = 0; I < argc; i++) {
        len += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // Add space between args and null terminator at the end
    }
    char *result = malloc(len);
    int pos = 0;
    for (int I = 0; I < argc; i++) {
        strcpy(&result[pos], argv[i]);
        pos += strlen(argv[i]);
        if (i != argc - 1) {
            result[pos++] = ' ';
        }
    }
    result[pos] = '\0';
    return result;
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    char *joined_args = join_args(argc, argv);
    printf("Joined arguments: %s\n", joined_args);
    free(joined_args);
    return 0;
}
```

The function `join_args()` takes `argc` (number of command-line arguments) and `argv` (array of strings containing the arguments), joins them with spaces, and returns a single null-terminated string that contains all the arguments. The returned string is dynamically allocated using `malloc()`, so it needs to be freed after use.

In the `main()` function, we call `join_args()` with the actual `argc` and `argv` values obtained from the compiler, print the result, and then free the memory allocated by `join_args()`. This ensures that we don't have any memory leaks in our program.

