In terms of supporting multiple conversational paths, the chatbot can utilize a decision tree or a finite state machine (FSM) approach to manage the conversation flow. Here's an overview of how this might look like using an FSM:

1. Define the initial state - Welcome State
2. Define the transitions between states - based on user inputs and system events
3. Define the actions associated with each state - generate responses, update context, and move to the next state

For instance, let's say the chatbot has three primary intents - greetings, queries, and requests. Here's a high-level representation of the FSM:

```mermaid
graph TD;
  Start[Start] --> Welcome[Welcome State];
  Welcome --> Greeting[Greeting Intent];
  Greeting --> Query[Query Intent];
  Query --> Request[Request Intent];
  Request --> Confirmation[Confirmation Intent];
  Request --> Error[Error Intent];
  Query --> Error;
  Greeting --> Error;
  Request --> Cancel[Cancel Intent];
  Cancel --> End[End State];

  classDef system {"system", fill:#f8f8f8, color:black};
  class Greeting, Query, Request, Confirmation, Error, Cancel, End {"user", fill:#eaf2d3, stroke:#cacfd2};

  subgraph System {
    system["System"]
    System -- Trigger --> Greeting : User Input;
    System -- Trigger --> Query : User Input;
    System -- Trigger --> Request : User Input;
    System -- Trigger --> Cancel : User Input;
    System -- Trigger --> Error : System Event;
    System -- Trigger --> Confirmation : System Event;
  }

  subgraph User {
    Greeting["Greeting"]
    Greeting --> Welcome : Initial State;
    Greeting --> Query : Navigate To Query State;
    Greeting --> Request : Navigate To Request State;
    Greeting --> Cancel : Navigate To Cancel State;
    Greeting --> Error : Handle Error;

    Query["Query"]
    Query --> Request : Navigate To Request State;
    Query --> Confirmation : Confirm Request;

