Certainly! Here's an implementation of the binary search algorithm in C++:

```cpp
int binarySearch(int arr[], int left, int right, int x) {
    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

        // Check if element is present at mid
        if (arr[mid] == x)
            return mid;

        // If element is smaller than mid, then it can only be present in left subarray
        if (arr[mid] > x)
            right = mid - 1;

        // Else the element can only be present in right subarray
        else
            left = mid + 1;
    }

    // Element was not present
    return -1;
}
```

This function takes four parameters: the sorted array `arr`, the initial left index `left`, the initial right index `right`, and the target value `x`. It returns the index of the found element or -1 if the element is not present in the array. The function works by repeatedly dividing the search interval in half until the target value is found or the search interval is empty.

One lesser-known factor that contributes to the efficiency of this algorithm is the use of integer division (`/`) instead of floating-point division (`/`) when calculating the middle index `mid`. This ensures that `mid` is always rounded down to the nearest integer, which is desirable in this case because we want the search interval to be halved exactly. Otherwise, if we used floating-point division, `mid` might be slightly above or below the actual midpoint, which could lead to unnecessary iterations and reduce the overall performance of the algorithm.

